<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            20.12.480  King County Flood
Management Plan - adopted - policies.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>  The 2024 King County Flood Management Plan,
as shown in Attachment A to Ordinance 19820* is adopted as a functional plan to
guide King County's river and floodplain management program and to meet the
intent of the natural environment, and facilities and services policies of the
King County Comprehensive Plan.  As an amplification and augmentation of the
King County Comprehensive Plan, the 2024 King County Flood Management Plan constitutes
official county policy with regard to river and floodplain management in King
County.  Site-specific projects and program activities are intended to function
at the level of planning document and do not assume that the nature and scope
of each of the described projects are the final project or action.  The
proposed projects and other activities are not intended to substitute for the
site-specific analysis to determine what is required for each of the site-specific
capital projects that will be recommended and adopted as part of an biennial
capital improvement plan.  The priority, scope, nature and cost of the proposed
projects or actions may change as the hydraulic, engineering and geotechnical
conditions at each site are analyzed in greater detail, and as engineering
alternatives are developed, analyzed, reviewed and negotiated with federal,
state, local and tribal agencies and affected property owner or owners. 
However, while the plan sets forth what the county currently believes are best
practices, nothing in this plan creates or precludes the creation of new land
use requirements, laws or regulations.  (Ord. 19820 § 8, 2024:  Ord. 17697 </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> 1, 2013:  Ord. 15673 §
2, 2007:  Ord. 11112 § 1, 1993).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>*Available in the King
County Archives.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>20.12.485  Potential Annexation
Area Process.</b>  The potential annexation area process involves two separate
determinations:  the boundaries of the PAA's, and how services within those PAA's
are to be provided.  Executive staff negotiating these issues with the relevant
cities shall assure that residents and community groups in the affected areas
are given meaningful opportunities to participate in the negotiation process. 
Executive staff shall keep councilmembers in whose districts the PAA's
are located apprised of public participation processes undertaken by the
executive, and provide them with notice of any public meetings on PAA's
well in advance of the meetings.  If executive staff relies on city planning
processes in which the county has not participated, documentation of the
processes used by the cities shall be transmitted with any recommended PAA
agreements.  Further, executive staff shall provide summaries of the processes
it has used to achieve public participation in any transmittals of PAA
agreements forwarded to the council.  (Ord. 12061 § 5, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc441658349"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>20.18  PROCEDURES
FOR AMENDMENT OF COMPREHENSIVE PLAN OR OF DEVELOPMENT REGULATIONS-PUBLIC
PARTICIPATION</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.020      Purpose.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.030      General
procedures.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.040      Site-specific
land use map or shoreline master program map amendment classification.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.050      Site-specific
land use map and shoreline master program map amendments initiation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.055      Site-specific
land use map amendment review standards and transmittal procedures.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.056      Shoreline
environment redesignation (in effect in the shoreline jurisdiction until
fourteen days after the state Department of Ecology approval of Ordinance
19881, Section 48).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.056      Shoreline
environment redesignation (in effect everywhere except the shoreline
jurisdiction, where it will take effect fourteen days after state Department of
Ecology approval of Ordinance 19881, Section 48).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.057      Redesignation
and applications.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.058      Redesignations
initiated by motion.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.060      Ten-year
cycle process - additional updates for capital improvement program,
transportation needs report, and school capital facility plans.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.070      Annual
update cycle process - proposed amendments in annual, midpoint, or ten-year
update.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.080      Subarea
plan adoption.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.090      Development
regulations proposal - notice.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.100      Description
of the amendments.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.110      Notice
of public hearing for Comprehensive Plan amendments and development
regulations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.130      Amendment
process following the conclusion of the public review and comment period.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.140      Provision for receipt, review of,
and response to the docket.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.150      Provision for notice of intent to
amend, and postadoption notice.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.160      Public participation program -
basic elements - exemptions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.170      The four-to-one program -
purpose- process - triparty agreement required - term conservation easement,
permanent dedication of natural area - referral to entities for recommendations
- prohibitions on development proposals or activities if adjacent to incorporated
area.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.180      The four-to-one program –
criteria for amending the urban growth area to achieve open space.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.190      The four-to-one program -
proposals adding ten or more dwelling units.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>         20.18.200      Effective date of amendments that
add land to Urban Growth Area, removes land from agricultural production
district or forest production district, or removes land from the mineral
resources map - notification to clerk of the council.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.020  Purpose.</b>
 The purpose of this chapter is to establish the procedures and review criteria
for amending the county’s comprehensive plan and development regulations and
providing for public participation.  Amendments to the comprehensive plan are
the means by which the county may modify its twenty-year plan for land use,
development or growth policies in response to changing county needs or
circumstances.  All plan and development regulation amendments will be reviewed
in accordance with the state Growth Management Act (GMA) and other applicable
state laws, the countywide planning policies, the adopted King County
Comprehensive Plan, and applicable capital facilities plans.  All plan and
development regulation amendments will be afforded appropriate public review
pursuant to the provisions of Ordinance 13147.  (Ord. 13147 § 18, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.030  General
procedures.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  The King
County Comprehensive Plan shall be amended in accordance with this chapter,
which, in compliance with RCW 36.70A.130(2), establishes a public participation
program whereby amendments are considered by the council no more frequently
than once a year as part of the update schedule established in this chapter,
except that the council may consider amendments more frequently to address:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Emergencies,
only after public notice and an opportunity for public testimony, commensurate
with the nature of the emergency, in the same manner as an emergency ordinance
under Section 230.30 of the King County Charter;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  An
appeal of the plan filed with the Central Puget Sound Growth Management
Hearings Board or with the court;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  The
initial adoption of a subarea plan, which may amend the Urban Growth Area
boundary only to redesignate land within a joint planning area;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  An
amendment of the capital facilities element of the Comprehensive Plan that
occurs in conjunction with the adoption of the county budget under K.C.C.
4A.100.010; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  The
adoption or amendment of a shoreline master program under chapter 90.58 RCW.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Every year
the Comprehensive Plan may be updated to address technical updates and
corrections, to adopt subarea plans, and to consider amendments that do not
require substantive changes to the Comprehensive Plan or subarea plans policy
language or do not require changes to the Urban Growth Area boundary, except as
allowed in Comprehensive Plan chapter 12.  The review may be referred to as the
annual update.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Every tenth
year beginning in 2024, the county shall complete a comprehensive review of the
Comprehensive Plan in order to update it as appropriate and to ensure continued
compliance with the GMA.  This review may provide for a cumulative analysis of
the twenty-year plan based upon official population growth forecasts,
benchmarks and other relevant data in order to consider substantive changes to the
Comprehensive Plan and changes to the Urban Growth Area boundary.  The
comprehensive review shall follow the schedule established in K.C.C. 20.18.060
and may be referred to as the ten-year update.  The Urban Growth Area boundaries
shall be reviewed in the context of the ten-year update and in accordance with
countywide planning policy FW-1 and RCW 36.70A.130.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.1.  At the
midpoint of the ten-year update process, a limited update to the Comprehensive
Plan to address time-sensitive issues before to the next ten-year update, may
be authorized by motion.  The update may be referred to as the midpoint
update.  The midpoint update may include those substantive changes to the
Comprehensive Plan and amendments to the Urban Growth Area boundary that are
identified in the scope of work.  The midpoint update may also include
additions or amendments to the Comprehensive Plan Workplan related to a topic
identified in the scope of work.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The
motion shall specify the scope of the midpoint update, and identify that the
resources necessary to accomplish the work are available.  A fiscal note for
the scope of the midpoint update shall be provided to the council by the
executive within fifteen business days of introduction of the proposed motion. 
If the executive determines an additional appropriation is necessary to
complete the midpoint update, the executive may transmit an ordinance
requesting the additional appropriation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=X-NONE
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>             
3.  If the executive proposes a midpoint update, the executive shall transmit
to the council by the last business day in March two years before the midpoint
year of the ten-year update </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>schedule</span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> <span lang=X-NONE>a
proposed motion specifying the scope of work for the midpoint update.  The
council shall have until June 30 of that year, to adopt a motion specifying the
scope of work initiating a midpoint update, either as transmitted or amended,
or as introduced or amended.  If the motion is approved by June 30, the scope
shall proceed as established by the approved motion.  In the absence of council
approval by June 30, the executive shall proceed to implement the scope as
transmitted.  If such a motion is adopted, the executive shall transmit a
midpoint update by the last business day of June of the following year after
adoption of the motion.  The council shall have until June 30 of the following
year after transmittal to adopt a midpoint update.</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  The
executive shall seek public comment on the Comprehensive Plan and any proposed Comprehensive
Plan update in accordance with the procedures in K.C.C. 20.18.160 before making
a recommendation, which shall include publishing a public review draft of the
proposed Comprehensive Plan update, in addition to conducting the public review
and comment procedures required by SEPA.  The public shall be afforded at least
one official opportunity to record public comment before the transmittal of a
recommendation by the executive to the council.  County-sponsored councils and
commissions may submit written position statements that shall be considered by
the executive before transmittal and by the council before adoption, if they
are received in a timely manner.  The executive's recommendations for changes
to policies shall include the elements listed in <span style='text-transform:
uppercase'>c</span>omprehensive <span style='text-transform:uppercase'>p</span>lan
policy I-108.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  Proposed
amendments to the <span style='text-transform:uppercase'>c</span>omprehensive <span
style='text-transform:uppercase'>p</span>lan shall be accompanied by any
development regulations or amendments to development regulations, including
area zoning, necessary to implement the proposed amendments.  (Ord. 19881 § 46,
2024:  Ord. 19146 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 8, 2020:  Ord. 18810 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
9, 2018:  Ord. 18623 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 7, 2017:  Ord. 18427 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
7, 2016:  Ord. 18183 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>2, 2015:  Ord. 17485 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
8, 2012:  Ord. </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>17416 § 9, 2012: </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> Ord. 16985 </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>5,
2010:  Ord. 16263 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 3, 2008:  Ord. 14047 § 1, 2001:  Ord. 13147 § 19, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>     </span><b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>20.18.040 
Site-specific land use map amendments - consideration - initiation -
classification.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A. 
Site-specific land use map amendments may be considered during the annual,
midpoint, or ten-year update, depending on the degree of change proposed.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Site-specific
land use map amendments that do not require substantive change to Comprehensive
Plan or subarea plan language and that do not alter the Urban Growth Area
boundary, except to correct mapping errors, may be initiated by either the
county or a property owner for consideration in the annual update.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  The
following categories of site-specific land use map amendments may be initiated
by either the county or a property owner for consideration in the ten-year update
or midpoint update:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Amendments
that could be considered in the annual update;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Amendments
that require substantive change to <span style='text-transform:uppercase'>c</span>omprehensive
<span style='text-transform:uppercase'>p</span>lan policy language; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Amendments
to the Urban Growth Area boundary.  (Ord. 19881 § 47, 2024:  Ord. 18810 </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>10,
2018:  Ord. 16985 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>6, 2010:  Ord. 14047 § 2, 2001:  Ord. 13147 § 20, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.050 
Site-specific land use map and shoreline master program map amendments
initiation.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A. 
Site-specific land use map and shoreline master program map amendments are
legislative actions that may be initiated by property owner application, by
council motion or by executive proposal.  All site-specific land use map and
shoreline master program map amendments must be evaluated by the hearing
examiner before adoption by the council in accordance with this chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  If
initiated by council motion, the motion shall refer the proposed site-specific
land use map or shoreline master program map amendment to the department of </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>local
services, permitting division, review</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> for preparation of a
recommendation to the hearing examiner.  The motion shall also identify the
resources and the work program required to provide the same level of review
accorded to applicant-initiated amendments.  An analysis of the motion’s fiscal
impact shall be provided to the council before adoption.  If the executive
determines that additional funds are necessary to complete the work program,
the executive may transmit an ordinance requesting the appropriation of
supplemental funds.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  If
initiated by executive proposal, the proposal shall refer the proposed
site-specific land use map or shoreline master program map amendment to the
department of </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>local services, permitting division,</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
for preparation of a recommendation to the hearing examiner.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  If
initiated by property owner application, the property owner shall submit a
docket request for a site-specific land use map or shoreline master program map
amendment to the department of </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>local services, permitting division,</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
for preparation of a recommendation to the hearing examiner.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  A
shoreline redesignation initiated by an applicant must include the following
information in addition to the requirements in this section:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1. 
Applicant information, including signature, telephone number and address;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The
applicant's interest in the property, such as owner, buyer or consultant; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Property
owner concurrence, including signature, telephone number and address.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  All
proposed site-specific land use map or shoreline master program map amendments,
whether initiated by property owner application, by council motion or by
executive proposal shall include the following:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Name and
address of the owner or owners of record;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2. 
Description of the proposed amendment;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Property
description, including parcel number, property street address and nearest cross
street;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  County
assessor's map outlining the subject property; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  Related
or previous permit activity.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  Upon
initiation of a site-specific land use map or shoreline master program map
amendment, an initial review conference shall be scheduled by the department of
</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>local services, permitting division</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>.  The owner or owners of
record of the property shall be notified of and invited to attend the initial
review conference.  At the initial review conference, the department of local
services, permitting division, shall review the proposed amendment’s
consistency with applicable county policies or regulatory enactments including
specific reference to Comprehensive Plan policies, countywide planning policies
and state Growth Management Act requirements.  The proposed amendment will be
classified in accordance with K.C.C. 20.18.040 and the classification shall be
provided at the initial review conference or in writing to the owner or owners
of record within thirty days after the initial review conference.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  If a
proposed site-specific land use map or shoreline master program map amendment
is initiated by property owner application, the property owner shall, following
the initial review conference, submit the completed application including an
application fee and an environmental checklist to the department of </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>local
services, permitting division,</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> to proceed with review of the
proposed amendment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  If a
proposed site-specific land use map or shoreline master program map amendment
is initiated by council motion, following the initial review conference, the
council shall submit an environmental checklist to the department of </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>local
services, permitting division,</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> to proceed with review of the
proposed amendment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            G.  If a
proposed site-specific land use map or shoreline master program map amendment
is initiated by executive proposal, following the initial review conference,
the executive shall submit an environmental checklist to the department of </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>local
services, permitting division,</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> to proceed with review of the
proposed amendment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            H.  Following
the submittal of the information required by subsection E., F. or G. of this
section, the department of </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>local services, permitting division,</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
shall submit a report including an executive recommendation on the proposed
amendment to the hearing examiner within one hundred twenty days.  The
department </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>of local services, permitting division,</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
shall provide notice of a public hearing and notice of threshold determination
in accordance with K.C.C. 20.20.060.F., G. and H.  The hearing will be
conducted by the hearing examiner in accordance with K.C.C. 20.22.170. 
Following the public hearing, the hearing examiner shall prepare a report and
recommendation on the proposed amendment in accordance with K.C.C. 20.22.170. 
A compilation of all completed reports will be considered by the council in
accordance with K.C.C. 20.18.070.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            I.  A
property-owner-initiated docket request for a site-specific land use map or
shoreline master program map amendment may be accompanied by an application for
a zone reclassification to implement the proposed amendment, in which case
administrative review of the two applications shall be consolidated to the
extent practical consistent with this chapter and K.C.C. chapter 20.20.  The
council’s consideration of a site-specific land use map or shoreline master
program map amendment is a legislative decision that should be determined
before and separate from its consideration of a zone reclassification, which is
a quasi-judicial decision.  If a zone reclassification is not proposed in
conjunction with an application for a site-specific land use map or shoreline
master program map amendment and the amendment is adopted, the property shall
be given potential zoning.  A zone reclassification in accordance with K.C.C.
20.20.020 is required in order to implement the potential zoning.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>J. 
Site-specific land use map or shoreline master program map amendments for which
a completed recommendation by the hearing examiner has been submitted to the
council by January 15 will be considered concurrently with the annual update to
the Comprehensive Plan.  Site-specific land use map or shoreline master program
map amendments for which a recommendation has not been issued by the hearing
examiner by January 15 shall be included in the next update following issuance
of the examiner's recommendation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            K.1.  An
amendment to a land use designation or shoreline environment designation for a
property may not be initiated unless at least three years have elapsed since
council adoption or review of the current designation for the property.  This
time limit may be waived by the executive or the council if the proponent
establishes that there exists either an obvious technical error or a change in
circumstances justifying the need for the amendment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  A waiver
by the executive shall be considered after the proponent has submitted a docket
request in accordance with K.C.C. 20.18.140.  The executive shall render a
waiver decision within forty-five days of receiving a docket request and shall
mail a copy of this decision to the proponent.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  A waiver
by the council shall be considered by motion.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            L.  A
shoreline master program map amendment and redesignation must meet the
requirements of K.C.C. 20.18.056, 20.18.057 and 20.18.058 and the Washington
state Shoreline Master Program Guidelines, chapter 173-26 WAC.  A shoreline
master program map amendment and redesignation must be approved by the
Washington state Department of Ecology.  (</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 19146 § 9,
2020:  Ord. 18791 § 147, 2018:  Ord. 18230 § 113, 2016:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
17420 § 82, 2012: </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> Ord. 16985 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ 7</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>,
2010:  Ord. 16552 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 1, 2009:  Ord. 14561 § 27, 2002:  Ord. 14047 § 3, 2001: 
Ord. 13147 § 21, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.055  Site-specific
land use map amendment review standards and transmittal procedures.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  All site-specific land use map
amendments, whether initiated by property owner application, by council motion,
or by executive proposal, shall be reviewed based upon the requirements of
Comprehensive Plan policy I-207, and must meet the following additional review
standards:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Consistency
with the policies, objectives and goals of the Comprehensive Plan, including
any applicable subarea plans, the countywide planning policies and the state
Growth Management Act;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Compatibility
with adjacent and nearby existing and permitted land uses; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Compatibility
with the surrounding development pattern.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Site-specific
land use map amendments for which recommendations have been issued by the
hearing examiner by January 15 shall be submitted to the executive and the
council by the hearing examiner by January 15.  The department will provide for
a cumulative analysis of these recommendations and such analysis will be
included in the annual March transmittal.  All such amendments will be
considered concurrently by the council committee charged with the review of the
Comprehensive Plan.  Following this review, site-specific land use map
amendments which are recommended by this committee will be incorporated as an
attachment to the adopting ordinance transmitted by the executive for
consideration by the full council.  Final action by the council on these
amendments will occur concurrently with the annual update to the Comprehensive Plan. 
(</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
color:black;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 19146 § 10, 2020:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
14047 § 4, 2001).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.18.056 
Shoreline environment redesignation (in effect in the shoreline jurisdiction
until fourteen days after the state Department of Ecology approval of Ordinance
19881, Section 48*).</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Shoreline
environments designated by the master program may be considered for
redesignation during the eight-year update or midpoint update.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  A
redesignation shall follow the process in K.C.C. 20.18.050.  (Ord. 16985 § 9,
2010:  Ord. 13687 § 2, 1999:  Ord. 12196 § 63, 1996:  Ord. 5734 § 17, 1981:  Ord.
3688 § 813, 1978.  Formerly K.C.C. 25.32.130).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>*Reviser's note:  </span></b><b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&quot;Sections 48, 270,
272, 273, 274, 275, 276, 277, 278, 279, and 280 of this ordinance and
amendments to King County Comprehensive Plan chapter six in Attachment A to
this ordinance take effect within the shoreline jurisdiction fourteen days
after the state Department of Ecology provides written notice of final action
stating that the proposal is approved, in accordance with RCW 90.58.090.  The
executive shall provide the written notice of final action to the clerk of the
council&quot;  (Ord. 19881 § 380).</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.18.056 
Shoreline environment redesignation (in effect everywhere except the shoreline
jurisdiction, where it will take effect fourteen days after state Department of
Ecology approval of Ordinance 19881, Section 48*).</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Shoreline
environments designated by the master program may be considered for
redesignation during the annual, midpoint, or ten-year update.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  A
redesignation shall follow the process in K.C.C. 20.18.050.  (Ord. 19881 § 48, 2024: 
Ord. 18810 § 11, 2018:  Ord. 16985 § 9, 2010:  Ord. 13687 § 2, 1999:  Ord.
12196 § 63, 1996:  Ord. 5734 § 17, 1981:  Ord. 3688 § 813, 1978.  Formerly
K.C.C. 25.32.130).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>*Reviser's note:  </span></b><b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&quot;Sections 48, 270,
272, 273, 274, 275, 276, 277, 278, 279, and 280 of this ordinance and
amendments to King County Comprehensive Plan chapter six in Attachment A to
this ordinance take effect within the shoreline jurisdiction fourteen days
after the state Department of Ecology provides written notice of final action
stating that the proposal is approved, in accordance with RCW 90.58.090.  The
executive shall provide the written notice of final action to the clerk of the
council&quot;  (Ord. 19881 § 380).</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            </span><b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>20.18.057 
Redesignation applications.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  In addition to the requirements
of K.C.C. 20.18.050, a shoreline redesignation initiated by an applicant must
include:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  A mitigation plan providing
for significant enhancement of the first one hundred feet adjacent to the
shoreline and improved habitat for species declared as endangered or threatened
under the Endangered Species Act, to the extent that the impacts of development
can be determined at the time of the proposed shoreline redesignation; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  A discussion of how the
proposed shorelines redesignation meets the criteria in K.C.C. 20.22.160.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  The examiner shall make a
recommendation to the council based on the criteria for review in K.C.C. 20.22.160. 
(</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
color:black'>Ord. 18230 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 114, 2016: 
</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>Ord. 16985 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ 11</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>, 2010:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 13687 § 3, 1999. 
Formerly K.C.C. 25.32.140).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Reviser’s note:  Ordinance
18230, Sections 162 and 163, apply to this section.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>20.18.058  Redesignations
initiated by motion.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            </span></b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>A.  In addition to the requirements in
K.C.C. 20.18.050, a council motion initiating a shoreline redesignation must be
accompanied by the information required by K.C.C. 20.18.057.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  A motion initiating a
site-specific shoreline redesignation must identify the resources and the work
program required to provide the same level of review accorded to an applicant-initiated
shoreline redesignation.  Before adoption of the motion, the executive shall
have the opportunity to provide an analysis of the motion’s fiscal impact.  If
the executive determines that additional funds are necessary to complete the
work program, the executive may transmit an ordinance requesting the
appropriation of supplemental funds.  The council may consider the supplemental
appropriation ordinance concurrently with the proposed motion referring the
shoreline redesignation proposal to the examiner.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  The examiner shall make a
recommendation to the council on the proposed site-specific shoreline
redesignation based on the criteria for review in K.C.C. 20.22.160.  (</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black'>Ord. 18230 <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 115, 2016:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
16985 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ 13</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>, 2010:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 13687 § 4, 1999. 
Formerly K.C.C. 25.32.150).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Reviser’s note:  Ordinance
18230, Sections 162 and 163, apply to this section.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.060  Ten-year
cycle process - additional updates for capital improvement program,
transportation needs report, and school capital facility plans.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Beginning
in 2030, and every ten years thereafter, the executive shall initiate the
ten-year update to the Comprehensive Plan required by K.C.C. 20.18.030.C.  The
ten-year update process shall occur as follows:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.a.  By
September 15, 2031, and every ten years thereafter, the executive shall
transmit to the council a proposed motion specifying the scope of work for the proposed
ten-year update to the <span style='text-transform:uppercase'>c</span>omprehensive
<span style='text-transform:uppercase'>p</span>lan in subsection A.2. of this
section.  The scoping motion shall include as an attachment to the motion the
following:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                (1)  topical
areas relating to amendments to policies, the land use map, implementing
development regulations, or any combination of those amendments that the
executive intends to consider for recommendation to the council; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                (2)  the
work program the executive intends to follow to accomplish State Environmental
Policy Act review and public participation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b.  The
council shall have until December 31 of that year to approve the motion.  In
the absence of council approval, the executive shall proceed to implement the scope
of work as proposed</span> <span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>in the motion transmitted by the executive.  If the
motion is approved, the scope of work shall proceed as established by the
approved motion.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            2.  Except as
otherwise provided in subsection B. of this section:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              a.  By June
30, 2033, and every ten years thereafter, the executive shall transmit to the
council a proposed ordinance for the ten-year <span style='text-transform:uppercase'>c</span>omprehensive
<span style='text-transform:uppercase'>p</span>lan update.  All transmittals
shall be accompanied by a public participation summary, identifying the methods
used by the executive to ensure early and continuous public participation in
the preparation of amendments; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b.  The
council shall have until June 30, 2034, and every ten years thereafter to adopt
the ten-year update to the Comprehensive Plan, in accordance with RCW
36.70A.130.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Separate
from ten-year Comprehensive Plan updates required in subsection A. of this
section:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Except
as otherwise provided in subsection B.2. of this section, in years when the
fiscal period is biennial, the capital improvement program, an update or
addendum where appropriate to the transportation needs report, and the school
capital facility plans for updates allowed under K.C.C. 21A.28.152.F. shall be:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a. 
transmitted by the executive to the council no later than transmittal of the
biennial budget;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b. 
adopted by the council in conjunction with the biennial budget; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2. 
Subsection B.1. of this section shall not apply to the transportation needs
report in years when a transmitted ten-year Comprehensive Plan update is being
reviewed by the council as required in subsection A.2. of this section; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  In years
when there is only a midbiennium review of the budget under K.C.C. 4A.100.010
or, under K.C.C. 4A.100.010.B., the fiscal period for some or all of the county
funds is on an annual basis, the capital improvement program and the school
capital facility plans shall be:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a. 
transmitted by the executive to the council by October 1; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b. 
adopted by the council no later than adoption of the midbiennium review</span> <span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>or
in conjunction with the annual budget.  (Ord. 19965 § 2, 2025:  Ord. 19881 § 49,
2024:  Ord. 19445 § 2, 2022:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;color:black;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 19146 § 11, 2020:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
18810 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ 12</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>, 2018:  Ord. 18183 </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ 3</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>,
2015:  Ord. </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>17416 § 10, 2012: </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> Ord. 14047 § 5, 2001: 
Ord. 13147 § 22, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.070  Annual
update cycle process - proposed amendments in annual, midpoint, or ten-year
update.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  The
executive shall transmit to the council the annual update by the last business
day of June, except that the capital improvement program, transportation needs
report, and the school capital facility plans shall be transmitted and adopted
in accordance with the schedules in K.C.C. 20.18.060.B.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Proposed
amendments, including site-specific land use map amendments, that are found to
require preparation of an environmental impact statement, shall be considered
for inclusion in the next annual, midpoint, or ten-year update following
completion of the appropriate environmental documents.  (Ord. 19881 § 50,
2024:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
color:black;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 19146 § 12, 2020:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
18810 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>13, 2018:  Ord. 18183 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>4,
2015:  Ord. </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>17416 § 11, 2012: </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> Ord. 14047 § 6, 2001: 
Ord. 13147 § 23, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.080  Subarea
plan adoption.  </b>Subarea plans for the community service area and potential
annexation area geographies shall be adopted consistent with the schedule
established in the Comprehensive Plan.  (Ord. 19881 § 51, 2024:  Ord. 18810 § 14,
2018:  Ord. 13147 § 24, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.090  Development
regulations proposal - notice.</b>  The executive</span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>may
propose development regulations to further implement the comprehensive plan,
consistent with the requirements of the Washington State Growth Management
Act.  Notice of proposed amendments to development regulations shall be
provided to the state and to the public pursuant to K.C.C. 20.18.150.  (Ord.
19881 § 52, 2024:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 18791 § 148, 2018: </span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
17420 § 83, 2012: </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> Ord. 13147 § 25, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.100  Description
of the amendments.</b>  All proposals for amendments to the comprehensive plan
or development regulations shall include a detailed description of the proposed
amendment in nontechnical terms.  This description will be made publicly
available by the responsible department or the council sponsor using one or
more methods provided in K.C.C. 20.18.160B and upon request.  This description
will be posted on the internet.  Internet posting of the description is
supplemental to other required notice, and the county's failure in any
particular case to provide notice via the internet shall not constitute a
procedural violation.  (Ord. 13147 § 26, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.110  Notice
of public hearing for Comprehensive Plan amendments and development
regulations.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Notice of
the time, place, and purpose of a public hearing before the council to consider
amendments to the Comprehensive Plan or development regulations shall at a
minimum be given at least thirty days before the hearing by the following
methods:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  One publication
in a newspaper of general circulation in the county.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  For land
use designation and zoning classification proposals only:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  one
additional publication in the area for which the area zoning is proposed, if
available;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b.  mailed
to affected property owners, appropriate to the scope of the proposal, whose
names appear on the rolls of the King County assessor and shall at a minimum
include owners of properties within five hundred feet of affected property, at
least twenty property owners in the vicinity of the property, and to any
individuals or organizations that have formally requested to the department of
local services, permitting division, the executive's office, or the council, to
be kept informed of applications in an identified area.  If the additional
publication referenced in subsection A.2.a. of this section is not available,
the mailing radius shall be extended to one thousand feet, and at least forty
property owners in the vicinity of the property.  The mail shall be postmarked
at least thirty days before the hearing.  If the county sends the mailed notice
by bulk mail, the certificate of mailing shall qualify as a postmark.  Failure
to notify any specific property owner shall not invalidate an area zoning
proceeding or any resulting reclassification of land; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                c.  posted
on the county website.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Notice for
site-specific land use map amendments shall also be provided in accordance with
K.C.C. 20.18.050.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  The county
shall endeavor to provide notices required by this section in nontechnical
language.  The notice shall indicate how the detailed description of the
ordinance required by K.C.C. 20.18.100 can be obtained by a member of the
public.  (Ord. 20082 § 83, 2026:  Ord. 19881 § 53, 2024:  Ord. 13147 § 27,
1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.18.130  Amendment
process following the conclusion of the public review and comment period.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  When the
council considers a change to an amendment to the comprehensive plan or
development regulation, and the change is proposed after the opportunity for
review and comment has concluded, an additional opportunity for review and
comment on the proposed change shall be provided before the council votes on
the proposed change.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  An
additional opportunity for public review and comment is not required if:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  An
environmental impact statement has been prepared under chapter 43.21C RCW for
the pending ordinance and the proposed change is within the range of
alternatives considered in the environmental impact statement;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The
proposed change is within the scope of the alternatives available for public
comment;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  The
proposed change only corrects typographical errors, corrects cross-references,
makes address or name changes or clarifies language of a proposed ordinance or
resolution without changing its effect;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  The
proposed change is to an ordinance making a capital budget decision as provided
in RCW 36.70A.120; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  The
proposed change is to an ordinance enacting a moratorium or interim control
adopted under RCW 36.70A.390.  (Ord. 13147 § 29, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.140  Provision
for receipt, review of, and response to the docket.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  In
accordance with RCW 36.70A.470, a docket containing written requests for
suggested Comprehensive Plan or development regulation amendments shall be
coordinated by the department.  The docket is the means either to suggest a
change or to identify a deficiency, or both, in the <span style='text-transform:
uppercase'>c</span>omprehensive <span style='text-transform:uppercase'>p</span>lan
or development regulation.  For the purposes of this section,
&quot;deficiency&quot; refers to the absence of required or potentially
desirable contents of the <span style='text-transform:uppercase'>c</span>omprehensive
<span style='text-transform:uppercase'>p</span>lan or development regulation
and does not refer to whether a development regulation addressed a project’s
probable specific adverse environmental impacts that could be mitigated in the
project review process.  Any interested party, including</span> <span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>permit
applicants, members of the public, and government agencies, may submit items to
the docket.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.1.  The
department shall provide a means by which members of the public may docket
written comments on the plan or on development regulations.  The department should
use public participation methods identified in K.C.C. 20.18.160 to publicize
the docket.  The department shall provide a mechanism for docketing amendments on
the county's website.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  All
docketed comments relating to the <span style='text-transform:uppercase'>c</span>omprehensive
<span style='text-transform:uppercase'>p</span>lan shall be reviewed by the
department and considered for an amendment to the <span style='text-transform:
uppercase'>c</span>omprehensive <span style='text-transform:uppercase'>p</span>lan.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Docketed
comments relating to development regulations shall be reviewed by the
appropriate county agency.  Those requiring a Comprehensive Plan amendment
shall be forwarded to the department and considered for an amendment to the
Comprehensive Plan.  Those not requiring a Comprehensive Plan amendment shall
be considered by the responsible county agency for amendments to the
development regulations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  The
deadline for submitting docketed comments is December 31 for consideration in
the update process for the following year.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  Except
as provided in subsection B.7. of this section:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  By the
last business day of April, the department shall issue an executive response to
all docketed comments.  Responses shall include: a classification of the
recommended changes as appropriate for the annual update, midpoint update, ten-year
update, or standalone development regulations update; and an executive
recommendation indicating whether the docketed items are to be included in the
next executive-recommended Comprehensive Plan update or a future standalone
development regulations update.  If the docketed changes will not be included
in the next executive transmittal, the department shall indicate the reasons
why, and shall inform the proponent that they may petition the council during the
legislative review process; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              b.  By the last
business day of April, the department shall forward to the council a report
including all docketed amendments and comments with an executive response.  The
report shall include a statement indicating that the department has complied
with the notification requirements in this section.  The executive shall attach
to the report copies of the docket requests and supporting materials submitted
by the proponents and copies of the executive response that was issued to the
proponents.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              6.  The
docket report shall be made available on the county's website.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              7.a  For
docket requests received between scoping and transmittal of midpoint and
ten-year updates, the executive shall include, as a supplemental document with
transmittal of the update, an analysis and recommendation for docket requests
received; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b.  For
docket requests received between transmittal and adoption of midpoint and
ten-year updates, that are not addressed in the update, the executive shall
include those requests in the next year's docket report.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              8.  After
receipt of the docket report, during the next available Comprehensive Plan
update, the council shall include all proponents of docketed requests in the
mailing list for agendas to all committee meetings in which the Comprehensive
Plan will be reviewed.  Docket proponents may petition the council to consider
docket changes that were not recommended by the executive.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  In
addition to the docket, the department shall provide opportunities for receiving
general public comments at any time, including as provided in K.C.C. 20.18.160. 
(Ord. 19881 § 54, 2024:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 18810 § 15, 2018:  Ord. 16263 § 4, 2008:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
15607 § 2, 2006:  Ord. 14047 § 8, 2001:  Ord. 13147 § 30, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.150  Provision
for notice of intent to amend, and postadoption notice.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Pursuant
to RCW 36.70A.106 and WAC 365-195-620, the responsible department or the
council sponsor of the amendment shall notify the state of its intent to adopt
amendments to the Comprehensive Plan or to development regulations, consistent
with RCW 36.70A.106.  When the state is notified, the department or the council
sponsor shall also provide notice to the public, using one or more methods
provided in K.C.C. 20.18.160.B., of the intent to amend the Comprehensive Plan or
development regulations, if such notice has not already been provided.  This
information will be posted on the internet.  Internet posting of the
information is supplemental to other required notice, and the county's
failure in any particular case to provide notice via the internet shall not
constitute a procedural violation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Within ten
days of enactment, the clerk of the council shall transmit to the state any
adopted plan, amendment to the Comprehensive Plan, or development regulation. 
Pursuant to RCW 36.70A.106, within ten days of enactment, the clerk of the
council shall provide published notice in a newspaper of general circulation of
adoption of or amendment to the Comprehensive Plan or any development
regulation.  The notice shall indicate how the detailed description of the
ordinance required by K.C.C. 20.18.100 can be obtained by a member of the
public.  (Ord. 19881 § 55, 2024:  Ord. 13147 § 31, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.160  Public
participation program - basic elements - exemptions.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  In
accordance with RCW 36.70A.140, the county shall provide for early and
continuous public participation in the development and amendment of the Comprehensive
Plan and any implementing development regulations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  The
county's public participation program shall at a minimum include the following
elements:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Broad
dissemination of upcoming opportunities for public participation, as they are
available;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Information
on public participation in the Comprehensive Plan process, a description of the
procedure and schedule for amending the Comprehensive Plan or implementing
development regulations, and information on how to use the docket;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Provision
for broad dissemination of the proposal and alternatives appropriate to the
scope and significance of the proposal.  The county shall make available to the
public printed and electronic information that clearly defines and visually
portrays, when possible, the range of options under consideration by the
county.  The information shall also include a description of any policy
considerations, the schedule for deliberation, opportunities for public
participation, information on the submittal and review procedures for written
comments, and the name, email address, and telephone number of the responsible
officials.  The methods employed to provide the information may include, but
are not limited to, the use of the following:  published notice in a newspaper
of general circulation and other appropriate publications; press releases;
notice to property owners and to members of the public or groups with a known
interest in the proposal; public television; the internet; transit advertising;
telephone information or comment lines; public review documents; posters;
agency newsletters and mailing list; and social media.  The county shall
endeavor to provide such notices in nontechnical language;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  Hosting,
speaking at, or attending public meetings to obtain comments from the public or
other agencies on a proposed plan, amendment to the Comprehensive Plan, or
implementing development regulation.  Public meeting means an informal meeting,
hearing, workshop, or other public gathering of people for the purpose of
obtaining public comments and providing opportunities for open discussion.  County-hosted
public meetings shall be appropriately noticed to the public and should be
broadly disseminated at least one week advance, except that noticing of
meetings held by the King County council are subject to council rules in K.C.C.
chapter 1.24.  A publicly available record of each county-hosted public meeting
should be maintained to include information about attendance, record of any
mailed notice, and a recording of the meeting or a summary of public comments;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  Other
methods of public engagement to solicit feedback about the proposal,
appropriate to the scope and significance of the proposal, such as surveys,
focus groups, partnering with community-based organizations, and online
engagement portals; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              6. 
County-provided mechanisms to enable public access to additional information. 
The county shall provide for publicly accessible records of all docketed
amendment requests and related background information during normal business
hours.  The public may seek assistance from the office of the ombuds to obtain
time sensitive information.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Along with
any executive's proposed Comprehensive Plan update, the executive shall
transmit to the council, as supplementary material:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  a public
participation summary, identifying the methods used by the executive to assure
early and continuous public participation in the preparation of update; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  a
summary of the proposal in non-technical language and translated into the top
six languages spoken in King County other than English.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  At the
beginning of the committee review process, the council shall develop a
committee review schedule with dates for committee meetings and any other
opportunities for public testimony and shall attach the review schedule to the
agenda whenever the Comprehensive Plan is to be reviewed.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  Errors in
exact compliance with the established procedures do not render the
Comprehensive Plan or development regulations invalid if the intent of the
procedures is met.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  Emergency
Comprehensive Plan amendments, as authorized by K.C.C. 20.18.030, are exempt
from the requirements of this section but still require some public notice and
an opportunity public testimony before adoption of the amendments.  (Ord. 19881
§ 56, 2024:  Ord. 13147 § 32, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.170  The
four-to-one program - purpose- process - triparty agreement required - term
conservation easement, permanent dedication of natural area - referral to
entities for recommendations - prohibitions on development proposals or
activities if adjacent to incorporated area.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  The
purpose of the four-to-one program is to create a contiguous band of natural
area to the regional open space system adjacent to the original Urban Growth
Area boundary, which was adopted in the 1994 King County Comprehensive Plan.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Proposals under
the four-to-one program:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Shall be
proposed via the docket process in K.C.C. 20.18.140, a scoping motion for a
midpoint or ten-year Comprehensive Plan update, or an area zoning and land use
study included in the public review draft of a Comprehensive Plan update; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Processed
as land use amendments to the Comprehensive Plan.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C. </span> <span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A
triparty agreement between the county, property owner, and city or town
affiliated for future annexation shall be required for all proposals.  The
triparty agreement shall:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Be
approved by ordinance by the legislative bodies of the county and the city or
town;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  For
county approval, be transmitted concurrent with transmittal of the executive's
proposed land use amendment and approved concurrent with council adoption of
the land use map amendment;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Require
the city or town to add the area proposed to be urban to the city's or town's
potential annexation area in the city's or town's comprehensive plan following
ratification of the Growth Management Planning Council's motion that makes a
recommendation on the proposal.  The approval of the proposal shall be
reflected in the Countywide Planning Policies, on both the generalized land use
categories map and the potential annexation area map; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  Specify
conditions including, but not limited to, restrictions on residential uses,
required minimum density, timing and sequencing of development, annexation
requirements, or requirements regarding the conservation easement.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  If the
countywide planning policy amendment that approves the proposal is not
ratified, the triparty agreement and four-to-one proposal shall be void and not
take effect, and the urban properties shall be redesignated to the rural area
land use designation and associated previous zoning during the next
Comprehensive Plan update.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  A term
conservation easement shall be placed on the natural area before the four-to-one
proposal is approved by the council.  The triparty agreement shall require the
permanent dedication of the natural area to King County before final plat
approval.  Dedication shall take the form of on-site or off-site fee simple,
off-site conservation easement, or on-site subdivision tract.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  Before
taking legislative action on the land use map amendment, proposals adjacent to
incorporated area or potential annexation areas shall be referred to the
following entities for recommendations: the affected city and special purpose
districts, such as sewer, water, and school districts, as applicable; and state
agencies, as applicable.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            G.  For
proposals adjacent to an incorporated area, conditions on the land use map
amendment and triparty agreement shall prohibit development proposals or
activities until the land is annexed into the adjacent city or town..  (Ord.
19881 § 57, 2024:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 18810 § 16, 2018:  </span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 17485 </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
9, 2012:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 16263 § 5, 2008:  </span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 14047 § 9,
2001).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.180  The
four-to-one program – criteria for amending the urban growth area to achieve
open space.</b>  Rural area land may be added to the Urban Growth Area in
accordance with the following criteria:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  A proposal
to add land to the Urban Growth Area under this program shall meet the
following criteria:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Land
added to the Urban Growth Area shall not interrupt an existing contiguous band
of public open space, parks, or watersheds along the Urban Growth Area boundary;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  A road
serving the land added to the urban area shall not be counted as part of the
required natural area;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Land
added to the Urban Growth Area for drainage facilities in support of its
development shall not require dedication of natural area;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  The
minimum depth of the natural area shall be:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  no
less than two hundred feet, unless the county determines that a smaller depth
is warranted due to the topography and critical areas on the site;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b.  generally
parallel the Urban Growth Area boundary; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                c.  configured
in such a way as to connect with open space on adjacent properties;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  The
on-site natural area shall include a fifty-foot landscaped buffer to surround
the new urban area.  The buffer shall include a mix of trees, shrubs, and
groundcover that are native to the area and that create a visual barrier or
separator to the new urban area.  The county may determine that a larger buffer
or different vegetation is warranted in order to restore the natural area or
habitat or would better protect natural resources and functions and land use
compatibility in the area;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              6.  The
minimum size of the property to be considered is twenty acres. Smaller parcels
may be combined to meet the twenty-acre minimum;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              7.  The land
to be retained as natural area is not needed for any facilities necessary to
support the urban development; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Proposals
shall be evaluated to determine those that are the highest quality, including,
but not limited to, consideration of the following:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Preservation
of fish and wildlife habitat, including wildlife habitat networks, and habitat
for endangered and threatened species;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Provision
of regional open space connections;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Protection
of wetlands, stream corridors, groundwater, and water bodies;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  Preservation
of unique natural, biological, cultural, historical, or archeological
resources;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  The size
of natural area dedication and connection to other open space along the Urban Growth
Area boundary; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              6.  The
ability to provide extensions of urban services to the redesignated urban
areas; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  The land
dedicated through the four-to-one program shall be preserved primarily as
natural areas.  Passive recreation, farming, or forestry may also be allowed as
an alternative to natural area.  The following additional uses may be allowed
only if located on a small portion of the natural area and only if these uses
are found to be compatible with the site's values and functions:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Trails;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Compensatory
mitigation of wetland losses on the urban portion of the proposal, consistent
with the Comprehensive Plan and K.C.C. chapter 21A.24; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Active
recreation uses not to exceed five percent of the total natural area, including
any off-site natural area dedicated for the proposal.  Support services and
facilities for the active recreation uses may only locate within the active
recreation area and shall not exceed five percent of the total acreage of the
active recreation area.  The entire natural area, including any active
recreation site, is a regional resource.  It shall not be used to satisfy the
on-site active recreation space requirements in K.C.C. 21A.14.180 for the urban
portion of the four-to-one property.  (Ord. 19881 § 58, 2024:  Ord. 17485 </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
10, 2012:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 16263 § 6, 2008:  </span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 15606 § 1,
2006:  Ord. 14047 § 10, 2001).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.190  The
four-to-one program - proposals adding ten or more dwelling units.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            For a
four-to-one proposal that adds ten or more dwelling units:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.1.  Thirty
percent of the total number of dwelling units shall be affordable units.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  For
proposals that include only owner-occupied market rate dwelling units, all
affordable dwelling units shall be:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a. 
owner-occupied dwelling units;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b. 
affordable to residents earning up to eighty percent of area median income; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                c. 
affordable for at least fifty years from the date of initial occupancy.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  For
proposals that include only rental dwelling units, all affordable dwelling
units shall be:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  rental
dwelling units;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b. 
affordable to residents earning up to sixty percent of area median income; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                c. 
affordable for the life of the project.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  For
proposals that include both homeownership and rental dwelling units:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  the
proportion of affordable rental dwelling units to affordable owner-occupied
dwelling units shall be identical to the proportion of market rate rental
dwelling units to market rate owner-occupied dwelling units; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b.  meet
the applicable affordability levels in subsections A.2. and A.3. of this
section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Accessory
dwelling units shall not be used to meet the requirements of this section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C. 
Developments subject to this section shall be subject to K.C.C. 21A.48.060 and
K.C.C. 21A.48.080. The county may modify or waive the standards in these
sections if the county determines them to not be applicable to a four-to-one
proposal.  (Ord. 19881 § 59, 2024).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.18.200  Effective
date of amendments that add land to Urban Growth Area, removes land from
agricultural production district or forest production district, or removes land
from the mineral resources map - notification to clerk of the council.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  The
effective date of an amendment that adds land to the Urban Growth Area, removes
land from the agricultural production district or forest production district,
or removes land from the mineral resources map shall be after the following:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1. 
Sixty-one days after the date of publication of notice of adoption of the
Comprehensive Plan; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  If a
petition for review to the growth management hearings board is timely filed,
upon issuance of the board's final order.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  The
effective date required in subsection A. of this section, and language
direction notification to the clerk of the council,  shall be specified in the
ordinance adopting the amendments.  (Ord. 19881 § 60, 2024).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc441658350"><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>20.20  PROCEDURES
FOR LAND USE PERMIT APPLICATIONS, PUBLIC NOTICE, HEARINGS AND APPEALS</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.010      Chapter purpose.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.015      Definition - manager.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.020      Classifications of land use
decision processes.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.030      Preapplication conferences.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.033      Application screening process
- purpose - standards - how deemed incomplete - possible additional
information, project modifications, studies.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.035      Community meeting required
under K.C.C. Title 21A before filing application - notice - meeting contents -
meeting information included with application - information for interested
persons for monitoring progress.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.040      Application requirements.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.060      Notice of application.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.062      Notice of Type I decisions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.070      Vesting.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.090      Notice of decision or
recommendation - appeals.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.100      Permit issuance - time periods
- application cancelation by division - suspension by applicant or for
nonresponse - time period exceptions - restart of time periods.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.105      Permit extension.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.120      Permit processing guides -
production - availability.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.130      Reports on permit review
timelines.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:1.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-1.5in'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.135      Permit review performance
reports - transmittal of ordinance that implements recommendations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.20.010  Chapter
purpose.</b>  The purpose of this chapter is to establish standard procedures
for land use applications, public notice, hearings, and appeals in King
County.  These procedures are designed to promote timely and informed public
participation in discretionary land use decisions; eliminate redundancy in the
application, permit review, hearing and appeal processes; provide for uniformity
in public notice procedures; minimize delay and expense; and result in
development approvals that implement the policies of the Comprehensive Plan.  (Ord.
19872 § 13, 2024:  Ord. 12196 § 8, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.20.015  Definition
- manager.</b>  Throughout this chapter unless the context clearly requires
otherwise, “manager” means the department of local services, permitting
division manager or designee.  (Ord. 19872 § 14, 2024).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            20.20.020 
Classifications of land use decision processes.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            </span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A. 
Land use decisions are classified into four types, based on who makes the
decision, whether public notice is required, whether a public hearing is
required before a decision is made, and whether administrative appeals are
provided.  The types of land use decisions are listed in subsection D. of this
section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Type 1
decisions are made by the manager of the department of </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>local
services, permitting division</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> (&quot;the division &quot;).  Type 1
decisions are administrative decisions.  An administrative appeal is not
provided.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Type 2
decisions require public notice and are made by the manager.  Type 2 decisions
are discretionary decisions that are subject to administrative appeal to the
hearing examiner.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Type 3
decisions require public notice and are quasi-judicial decisions made by the
hearing examiner following an open record hearing.  An administrative appeal is
not provided.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  Type 4
decisions require public notice and are site-specific quasi-judicial decisions
made by the council based on the record established by the hearing examiner,
after a recommendation by the division.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Except as
provided in K.C.C. 20.44.120.A.7., or unless otherwise agreed to by the
applicant, all Type 2, 3, and 4 decisions included in consolidated permit
applications that would require more than one type of land use decision process
may be processed and decided together, including any administrative appeals,
using the highest-numbered land use decision type applicable to the project
application.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Certain
development proposals are subject to additional procedural requirements beyond
the standard procedures established in this chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  Land use
decision types are classified as follows:</span></p>

<table class=MsoNormalTable border=1 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0
 style='margin-left:9.9pt;border-collapse:collapse;border:none'>
