  <td width=72 valign=top style='width:.75in;border:solid windowtext 1.0pt;
  border-top:none;padding:0in 5.4pt 0in 5.4pt;height:73.95pt'>
  <p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
  font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>TYPE 4<sup>1,4</sup></span></p>
  </td>
  <td width=162 valign=top style='width:121.5pt;border-top:none;border-left:
  none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;border-right:solid windowtext 1.0pt;
  padding:0in 5.4pt 0in 5.4pt;height:73.95pt'>
  <p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>
  </td>
  <td width=384 valign=top style='width:4.0in;border-top:none;border-left:none;
  border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;border-right:solid windowtext 1.0pt;
  padding:0in 5.4pt 0in 5.4pt;height:73.95pt'>
  <p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
  font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Site-specific zone reclassifications</span></p>
  <p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
  font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Site-specific shoreline environment
  redesignation</span></p>
  <p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
  font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Site-specific amendment or deletion of P
  suffix conditions</span></p>
  <p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
  font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Site-specific deletion of special district
  overlay</span></p>
  </td>
 </tr>
</table>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><sup><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>1  </span></sup><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>See K.C.C. 20.44.120.C. for provisions
governing procedural and substantive SEPA appeals and appeals of Type 2, 3, and
4 decisions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><sup><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>2  </span></sup><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>When an application for a Type 2 decision is
combined with other permits requiring Type 3 or 4 land use decisions under this
chapter, the examiner, not the manager, makes the decision.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><sup><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>3  </span></sup><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>A shoreline permit, including a shoreline
variance or conditional use, is appealable to the state Shorelines Hearings
Board and not to the hearing examiner.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><sup><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>4  </span></sup><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Approvals that are consistent with the
Comprehensive Plan may be considered by the council at any time.  Zone
reclassifications that are not consistent with the Comprehensive Plan require a
site-specific land use map amendment and the council's hearing and
consideration shall be scheduled with the amendment to the Comprehensive Plan
under K.C.C. 20.18.040 and 20.18.060.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            E.  The definitions in K.C.C.
21A.45.020 apply to this section.  (Ord. 19881 § 61, 2024:  Ord. 19872 § 15,
2024:  Ord. 19648 § 9, 2023:  Ord. 19291 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ 3, 2021
(expired May 25, 2022):  Ord. 19146 § 13, 2020:  </span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 19128 § 3, 2020:  Ord. 18791 § 150,
2018:  Ord. 18710 § 3, 2018:  Ord. 18683 § 36, 2018:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 18626 § 16, 2017: </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 17420 § 87, 2012: </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 17420 § 85, 2012
(Expired 12/31/2012): </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> Ord.
17029 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>
</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>5, 2011</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> (Expired 12/31/2012):  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 16263 <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 7, 2008:  </span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 15606 § 2, 2006:  Ord. 15170 <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 2, 2005:  Ord. 14449 § 2, 2002:  Ord.
14190 § 23, 2001:  Ord. 14047 § 11, 2001:  Ord. 13694 § 84, 1999:  Ord. 13147 §
33, 1998:  Ord. 13131 § 1, 1998:  Ord. 12878 § 2, 1997:  Ord. 12196 § 9, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.20.030  Preapplication
conferences.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A. </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>B.  Before filing a
permit application requiring a Type 4 decision or a land use decision required
to comply with the North Highline urban design standards in K.C.C. chapter 21A.09,
the applicant shall contact the division to schedule a preapplication
conference, which shall be held before filing the application.  The purpose of
the preapplication conference is to review and discuss the application
requirements with the applicant and provide comments on the development
proposal.  The preapplication conference shall be scheduled by the division, at
the request of an applicant, and shall be held within thirty days from the date
of the applicant's request.  The division shall assign a project manager following
the preapplication conference.  The manager may waive the requirement for a
preapplication conference if the manager determines the preapplication
conference is unnecessary for review of an application.  Nothing in this
section shall be interpreted to require more than one preapplication conference
or to prohibit the applicant from filing an application if the division is
unable to schedule a preapplication conference within thirty days following the
applicant's request.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  An applicant wishing to submit
a permit application more than one year following a mandatory preapplication
for the same permit application shall be required to schedule another
preapplication conference.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  At or subsequent to a
preapplication conference, the department may issue a preliminary determination
that a proposed development is not permissible under applicable county policies
or regulatory enactments.  In that event, the applicant shall have the option
to appeal the preliminary determination to the hearing examiner in the manner
provided for a Type 2 permit, as an alternative to proceeding with a complete
application.  Mailed and published notice of the appeal shall be provided for
as in K.C.C. 20.20.060.  (Ord. 19872 § 16, 2024:  Ord. 19687 § 2, 2023:  </span><span
lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>Ord. 17841 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span><span lang=DE
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>5,
2014</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>:</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> 
<span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 16950 </span></span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>7,
2010:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord.
16552 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>
2, 2009:  Ord. 13332 § 65, 1998:  Ord. 12196 § 10, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>20.20.033  Application screening
process - purpose - standards - how deemed incomplete - possible additional
information, project modifications, studies.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  The purpose of the application
screening process is to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  assist applicants in
preparing a code compliant permit application; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  reduce the division's need to
request additional information during the permit review process, which extends
the time period for permit review.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  Application screening is
subject to the following standards:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Applicants shall submit the
required permit application and application materials identified in K.C.C.
20.20.040 in order to initiate the application screening process;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Within twenty-eight days
following receipt of the permit application and materials, the division shall
notify the applicant that the application is complete or incomplete.  If the
application is incomplete, the notification shall specify what is necessary to
make the application complete.  To the extent known by the division, the
notification shall identify other agencies of local, state, regional, or
federal governments that may have jurisdiction over some aspects of the
development proposal;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  The application screening
process shall be deemed concluded under this section if the division does not
provide written notice to the applicant that the application is incomplete
within the twenty-eight-day period;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  If the application is found
to be incomplete and the applicant submits the additional information requested
by the division, the division shall notify the applicant in writing within
fourteen days of resubmittal whether the application is complete or whether
additional information specified by the division is necessary to make the
application complete.  The application shall be deemed complete if the division
fails to notify the applicant within fourteen days of resubmittal that the
application is incomplete;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              5.  The division may terminate
the application screening process if the applicant fails to submit the
additional information requested by the county within sixty days following
notification from the division that the application is incomplete; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              6.  Once all information
requested by the division has been provided by the applicant and screened by
the division, the division shall invoice the applicant for applicable permit
application fees for the permit application.  The division may terminate the
permit review process if the applicant fails to submit the applicable review
fees within sixty days of invoicing by the county.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  An application shall be deemed
incomplete if the development proposal requires amendment to the Comprehensive
Plan, amendment to a development regulation, zone reclassification, shoreline
environment redesignation, amendment or deletion of a P-suffix condition,
deletion of a special district overlay, or deletion of a Demonstration Project
Area, and they have not already been obtained.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D.  Although a permit application
is deemed complete for purposes of this chapter when it meets the procedural
submission requirements of the division and is sufficient for continued
processing, additional information may be required or project modifications may
be undertaken subsequently.  The determination of completeness shall not
preclude the division from requesting additional information or studies either
at the time of notice of completeness or subsequently if new or additional
information is required or changes in the proposed action occur, as determined
by the division.  (Ord. 19872 § 17, 2024).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>20.20.035  Community meeting
required under K.C.C. Title 21A before filing application - notice - meeting
contents - meeting information included with application - information for
interested persons for monitoring progress.</b>  When an applicant is required
by K.C.C. Title 21A to conduct a community meeting, under this section, before
filing of an application, notice of the meeting shall be given and the meeting
shall be conducted as follows:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  At least two weeks in advance,
the applicant shall:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Publish notice of the meeting
in the local paper and mail and email to the department; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Mail notice of the meeting to
all property owners within five hundred feet or at least twenty of the nearest
property owners, whichever is greater, as provided in K.C.C. 21A.26.170 of any
potential sites, identified by the applicant for possible development, to be
discussed at the community meeting.  The mailed notice shall, at a minimum,
contain a brief description and purpose of the proposal, approximate location
noted on an assessor map with address and parcel number, photograph or sketch
of any existing or proposed structures, a statement that alternative sites
proposed by the public can be presented at the meeting that will be considered
by the applicant, a contact name and telephone number to obtain additional
information and other information deemed necessary by the department of </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>local services,
permitting division</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>. 
Because the purpose of the community meeting is to promote early discussion,
applicants shall note any changes to the conceptual information presented in
the mailed notices when they submit applications;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  At the community meeting at
which at least one employee of the department of </span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>local services, permitting division</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>, assigned by the permitting
division manager or designee, shall be in attendance, the applicant shall
provide information relative to the proposal and any modifications proposed to
existing structures or any new structures and how the proposal is compatible
with the character of the surrounding neighborhood.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'>      <span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>C.  At time of application, the
applicant shall provide a list of meeting attendees and those receiving mailed
notice of the meeting and a record of the published meeting notice; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D.  The applicant shall, in the
notice required under subsection A.2. of this section, and at the community
meeting required under subsection B. of this section, advise that persons
interested in the applicant's proposal may monitor the progress of the permitting
of that proposal by contacting the department or by viewing the department's
website, the address of which will be provided in the notice and at the
community meeting.  (Ord. 19881 § 62, 2024:  </span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 18791 § 151, 2018: </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 17420 § 88, 2012: </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> Ord. </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>17416
§ 13, 2012: </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> Ord.
16950 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>
</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>10, 2010).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>20.20.040  Application
requirements.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  For the purposes of determining
the application of time periods and procedures adopted in this title, applications
for land use decisions shall be considered complete upon determination by the division
that the materials submitted contain the following:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  An application form provided
by the division and completed by the applicant;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Designation of who the
applicant is, except that this designation shall not be required as part of a
complete application for purposes of this section when a public agency or
public or private utility is applying for a permit for property on which the
agency or utility does not own an easement or right of way and the following
three requirements are met:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                a.  the name of the agency or
private or public utility is shown on the application as the applicant;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                b.  the agency or private or
public utility includes in the complete application an affidavit declaring that
notice of the pending application has been given to all owners of property to
which the application applies, on a form provided by the division; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                c.  the form designating who
the applicant is submitted to the division before permit approval;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.a.  A certificate of sewer
availability or site design approval for an on-site sewage system by public
health -Seattle &amp; King County, as required by K.C.C. Title 13; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                b.  If allowed under <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>K.C.C. </span>13.24.134.B. and the King County
Comprehensive Plan policies for a public school located on a RA zoned site, a
certificate of sewer availability and a letter from the sewer utility
indicating compliance with the tightline sewer provisions in the zoning code,
as required by K.C.C. chapter 13.24;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  If the development proposal
requires a source of potable water, a current certificate of water availability
consistent with K.C.C. chapter 13.24 or documentation of an approved well by
public health - Seattle &amp; King County;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              5.  A fire district receipt in
accordance with K.C.C. Title 17, if required by K.C.C. chapter 21A.40;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              6.  A site plan, except when
exempt under K.C.C. 16.02.255.  A site plan shall include:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                a.  identification of the
subject property;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                b.  scale of the drawings;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                c.  north arrow and legend;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                d.  location and dimensions of
the parcel, streets, and buildings;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                e.  easements and restrictions;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                f.  existing and proposed
structures;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                g.  existing and proposed
parking and driveway areas;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                h.  sewer connection or on-site
sewer system;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                i.  water connection or well
location with well radius;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                j.  building and structure
elevations;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                k.  past excavation, filled
areas, or cleared areas;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                l.  existing and proposed
contours two-foot intervals;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                m.  survey benchmark location;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                n.  datum;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                o.  existing and proposed
impervious surface;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                p.  critical areas, buffers,
and setback lines;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                q.  temporary erosion and
sediment control measures;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                r.  construction access;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                s.  existing and proposed
drainage facilities and flow control best management practices; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                t.  driveway and fire access;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              7.  Proof that the lot or lots to
be developed are recognized as a lot under K.C.C. Title 19A;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              8.  A critical areas affidavit,
if required by K.C.C. chapter 21A.24;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              9.  A completed environmental
checklist, if required by K.C.C. chapter 20.44;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              10.  Payment of application
review screening fees collectible under K.C.C. Title 27, if any;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              11.  A list of any permits or
decisions applicable to the development proposal that have been obtained before
filing the application or that are pending before the county or any other
governmental entity;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              12.  Certificate of future
connection from the appropriate purveyor for lots located within the urban area
that are proposed to be served by on-site or community sewage system and group
B water systems or private well, if required by K.C.C. 13.24.136 through
13.24.140;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              13.  A determination if drainage
review applies to the project under K.C.C. chapter 9.04 and, if applicable, all
drainage plans and documentation required by the Surface Water Design Manual
adopted under K.C.C. chapter 9.04. and to the extent known at the time of
application and when determined necessary by the manager, copies of any
required storm water adjustments.  Components of the drainage review and plans
and documentation that can only be completed during a seasonal window shall not
be required for purposes of a complete application;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              14.  Legal description of the
site;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>               15.  Approvals for any decisions
when deemed necessary by the manager, including but not limited to critical
area alteration exceptions, reasonable use exceptions, special use permits,
conditional use permits, zoning variances, road variances, or drainage standard
adjustment or drainage criteria exception; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              16.  For commercial site
development permits only, a phasing plan and a time schedule, if the site is
intended to be developed in phases or if all building permits will not be
submitted within three years.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  Additional complete application
requirements for the following applications are in the following sections of
the King County Code:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Clearing and grading permits,
K.C.C. 16.82.060;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Construction permits, K.C.C. 16.02.260;
and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  Subdivision applications,
short subdivision applications, and binding site plan applications, K.C.C.
19A.08.150.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  The manager may:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Specify the requirements of
the site plan required to be submitted for various permits; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Waive any of the specific
submittal requirements listed in this section that are determined to be
unnecessary for review of an application.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D.  The applicant shall declare
under penalty of perjury as to the accuracy of all information submitted for an
application.  (Ord. 19872 § 18, 2024:  Ord. 19485 § 205, 2022:  Ord. 18791 </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ 152,
2018:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord.
17539 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ 14, 2013:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 17485 </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
11, 2012: </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> Ord.
15606 § 3, 2006:  Ord. 15243 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 1,
2005:  Ord. 14788 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ 6</span>, 2003:  Ord.
14507 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span>5, 2002:  Ord. 13694 § 85,
1999:  Ord. 13190 § 13, 1998:  Ord. 12380 § 6, 1996:  Ord. 12196 § 11, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>20.20.060  Notice of
application.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  A notice of application shall
be provided to the public for land use applications as follows:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Type 2, 3, or 4 decisions;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Type 1 decisions subject to
SEPA, except where the division is not the SEPA lead agency for the project;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  As provided in subsection K.
of this section; </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  Type 1 decisions requiring a
community meeting under K.C.C. 20.20.035; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              5.  Where prescribed by ordinance.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  Notice of the application shall
be provided by the division within fourteen days following the division
determination that the application is complete.  The public comment period shall
be for fourteen days following issuance of the notice of application, except
that shoreline permits shall have a comment period of thirty days.  The public
comment period shall commence on the day the notice of application is published
as provided for in subsection I. of this section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  If the county has made a
determination of significance (&quot;DS&quot;) under chapter 43.21C RCW before
the issuance of the notice of application, the notice of the DS shall be
combined with the notice of application and the scoping notice.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D.  Unless the mailed notice of
application is by a post card as provided in subsection E. of this section, the
notice of application shall contain the following information:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  The file number;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  The name of the applicant;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  The date of application, the
date of the notice of completeness and the date of the notice of application;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  A description of the project,
the location, a list of the permits included in the application, and the publicly-accessible
site where the application and any environmental documents or studies can be
reviewed;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              5.  A site plan on eight and
one-half by fourteen inch paper, if applicable;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              6.  The procedures and deadline
for filing comments, requesting notice of any required hearings and any appeal
procedure;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              7.  The date, time, place, and
type of hearing, if applicable and scheduled at the time of notice;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              8.  The identification of other
permits not included in the application to the extent known;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              9.  The
identification of existing environmental documents that evaluate the proposed
project; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              10.  A
statement of the preliminary determination, if one has been made, of those
development regulations that will be used for project mitigation and of
consistency with applicable county plans and regulations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  If mailed
notice of application is made by a post card, the notice of application shall
contain the following information:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  A
description of the project, the location, a list of the permits included in the
application and any environmental documents or studies can be reviewed;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The name
of the applicant;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  The date
of application, the date of the notice of completeness and the date of the
notice of application;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  If the division
has made a decision or recommendation on the application, the decision or
recommendation made;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  The
applicable comment and appeal dates and the date, time, place and type of
hearing, if applicable;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              6.  A website
address that provides access to project; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              7.  The division
contact name, telephone number, and email address;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  Notice
shall be provided in the following manner:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Posted
at the project site as provided in subsections G. and J. of this section;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Mailed
by first class mail as provided in subsection H. of this section; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Published
as provided in subsection I. of this section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            G.  Posted
notice for a proposal shall consist of one or more notice boards posted by the
applicant within fourteen days following the division's determination of
completeness as follows:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  A single
notice board shall be posted for a project.  This notice board may also be used
for the posting of the notice of decision and notice of hearing and shall be
placed by the applicant:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  at the
midpoint of the site street frontage or as otherwise directed by the division
for maximum visibility;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b.  five
feet inside the street property line except when the board is structurally
attached to an existing building, but a notice board shall not be placed more
than five feet from the street property without approval of the division;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                c.  so
that the top of the notice board is between seven to nine feet above grade;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                d.  where
it is completely visible to pedestrians; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                e.  comply
with sight distance requirements of K.C.C. 21A.12.210 and the King County road
standards adopted under K.C.C. chapter 14.42;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Additional
notice boards may be required when:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  the
site does not abut a public road;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b.  a
large site abuts more than one public road; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                c.  the division
determines that additional notice boards are necessary to provide adequate
public notice;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Notice
boards shall be:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  maintained
in good condition by the applicant during the notice period through the time of
the final county decision on the proposal, including the expiration of any
applicable appeal periods, and for decisions that are appealed, through the
time of the final resolution of any appeal; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b.  in
place at least twenty-eight days before the date of any required hearing for a
Type 3 or 4 decision, or at least fourteen days following the division's
determination of completeness for any Type 2 decision; </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  Removal
of the notice board before the end of the notice period may be cause for
discontinuance of county review until the notice board is replaced and remains
in place for the specified notice period;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  An
affidavit of posting shall be submitted to the division by the applicant within
fourteen days following the division's determination of completeness to allow
continued processing of the application by the division;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              6.  Notice
boards shall be constructed and installed in accordance with subsection G. of
this section and any additional specifications promulgated by the department
under K.C.C. chapter 2.98, rules of county agencies; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              7.  The manager
may waive the notice board requirement for a development proposal located in an
area with restricted access, an area that is not served by public roads, or in
other circumstances the manager determines make the notice board requirement
ineffective in providing notice to those likely to be affected by the
development proposal.  In such cases, the manager shall require alternative
forms of notice under subsection M. of this section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            H.  Mailed
notice for a proposal shall be sent by the division within fourteen days after
the division's determination of completeness:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  By first
class mail to owners of record of property in an area within five hundred feet
of the site.  The area shall be expanded when the division determines it is necessary
to send mailed notices to at least twenty different property owners;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  To any
city with a utility that is intended to serve the site;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  To the Washington
state Department of Transportation, if the site adjoins a state highway;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  To the
affected Indian tribes;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  To any
agency or community group that the division may identify as having an interest
in the proposal;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              6.  Be
deemed satisfactory despite the failure of one or more owners to receive mailed
notice;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              7.  For
preliminary plats only, to all cities within one mile of the proposed
preliminary plat, and to all airports within two miles of the proposed
preliminary plat;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              8.  Where
King County and a city have adopted either a memorandum of understanding or a
potential annexation boundary agreement, or both, the manager shall ensure that
the city receives notice of all applications for development subject to this
chapter and shall respond specifically in writing to any comments on proposed
developments subject to this title.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            I.  The notice
of application shall be published by the division within fourteen days after
the division's determination of completeness on the division website and in a
newspaper of general circulation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            J.  Unless
waived under subsection G.7. of this section, posted notice for approved formal
subdivision engineering plans, clearing or grading permits subject to SEPA or
building permits subject to SEPA, shall be a condition of the plan or permit
approval and shall consist of a single notice board posted by the applicant at
the project site, before construction as follows:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Notice
boards shall comport with the size and placement provisions identified for
construction signs in K.C.C. 21A.20.120.B;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Notice
boards shall include the following information:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  permit
number and description of the project;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b.  projected
completion date of the project;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                c.  a
contact name and phone number for both the division and the applicant;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                d.  a division
contact number for complaints after business hours; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                e.  hours
of construction, if limited as a condition of the permit;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Notice
boards shall be maintained in the same manner as identified in subsection F of
this section; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  Notice
boards shall remain in place until final construction approval is granted. 
Early removal of the notice board may preclude authorization of final
construction approval.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            K.  Posted and
mailed notice for any permit application for the establishment of the uses
listed in 1. through 3. of this subsection, consistent with this section shall
be provided to any property owner of record and to the councilmember in whose
district the proposal is located, except that this subsection K. shall not
apply if the proposed use is protected under the Fair Housing Act:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Offender
self-help agencies under SIC Industry 8322;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Parole
offices under SIC Industry 8322; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Halfway
homes for delinquents and offenders</span> <span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>under SIC Industry 8361</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>L.  In addition to notice
required by subsection F. of this section, the division may provide additional
notice by any other means determined by the division as necessary to provide
notice to persons or entity who may be affected by a proposal.  (Ord. 19872 § 19,
2024:  Ord. 18683 § 37, 2018:  Ord. 17539 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ 15, 2013:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
17191 § 16, 2011:  Ord. 16950 § 8, 2010:  Ord. 16552 § 3, 2009:  Ord. 13694 §
86, 1999:  Ord. 13573 § 1, 1999:  Ord. 13555 § 2, 1999:  Ord. 13131 § 2, 1998: 
Ord. 13097 § 1, 1998:  Ord. 12884 § 1, 1997:  Ord. 12196 § 13, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.20.062 
Notice of Type I decisions.</b>  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Not later than January 1, 2013, the department
shall provide public notice of Type 1 decisions for which a notice of
application is not otherwise required under K.C.C. 20.20.060.  The public
notice may be provided electronically.  The notice provided <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>under</span> this section shall be considered
supplementary to any other notice requirements and shall be deemed satisfactory
despite the failure of one or more individuals to receive notice.  (Ord. 17267
§ 1, 2012:  Ord. 16950 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 9, 2010).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.20.070  Vesting.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A. 
Applications for Type 1, 2, and 3 land use decisions, except those which seek
variance from or exception to land use regulations and substantive and
procedural SEPA decisions shall be considered under the zoning and other land
use control ordinances in effect on the date a complete application is filed
meeting all of the requirements of this chapter.  The department's
issuance of a notice of complete application as provided in this chapter, or
the failure of the department to provide such a notice as provided in this
chapter, shall cause an application to be conclusively deemed to be vested as
provided herein.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Supplemental
information required after vesting of a complete application shall not affect
the validity of the vesting for such application.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Vesting of
an application does not vest any subsequently required permits, nor does it
affect the requirements for vesting of subsequent permits or approvals.  (Ord.
12196 § 14, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.20.090  Notice
of decision or recommendation - appeals.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  In
accordance with K.C.C. 20.20.100, the department shall provide notice of:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Final Type
1 decisions subject to SEPA, including the threshold determination, if any;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Type 2
decisions; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Type 3
and 4 recommendations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  The notice
shall include the applicable procedures for either an administrative appeal to,
or further consideration by, the examiner.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  The notice
shall be provided to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  The
applicant;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  If
required by SEPA, the Department of Ecology and to agencies with jurisdiction as
defined in chapter 197-11 WAC;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  If
required by chapter 90.58 RCW, the Department of Ecology and the Attorney
General;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  Any
person who, before the decision or recommendation, had requested notice of the
decision or recommendation from, or submitted comments to, the department; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  Owners
of record of property in an area within five hundred feet of the site.  The
area shall be expanded when the department determines it is necessary to send
mailed notices to at least twenty different property owners.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  Except for
decisions regarding shoreline substantial development permits, shoreline
variances and shoreline conditional uses, which are only appealable to the
state Shorelines Hearings Board, any administrative appeal or further
consideration by the examiner is subject to K.C.C. chapter 20.22.  (Ord. 19881
§ 63, 2024:  Ord. 18230 § 116, 2016:  Ord. 16552 § 4, 2009:  Ord. 13573 § 2,
1999:  Ord. 13131 § 3, 1998: Ord. 13097 § 2, 1998:  Ord. 12196 § 16, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>20.20.100  Permit issuance -
time periods - application cancelation by division - suspension by applicant or
for nonresponse - time period exceptions - restart of time periods.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  The period to issue a final
decision for each type of complete land use application or project type subject
to this chapter should not exceed the following time periods, except as
modified by this section:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  For land use applications
that do not require public notice under K.C.C. 20.20.060, the division shall
issue a final decision within sixty-five days of the determination of
completeness;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  For land use applications
that require public notice under K.C.C. 20.20.060, the division must issue a
final decision within one hundred days of the determination of completeness;
and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  For land use applications
that require public notice under K.C.C. 20.20.060 and a public hearing, the
division must issue a final decision within one hundred seventy days of the
determination of completeness.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  The time periods for permit
review established in subsection A. of this section and as modified by
subsections C. through K. of this section, shall be extended by seventy-five
percent if a development proposal or application:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Requires approval of a road
variance under K.C.C. Title 14, or drainage standard adjustment or drainage
criteria exception under K.C.C. Title 9;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Is for a variance, critical
area alteration exception, or reasonable use exception under K.C.C. Title 21A;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  Is granted concurrent review
with other permit applications applicable to the development; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  Is for a development proposal
to install permanent stabilization measures to replace any structures or
grading done as an emergency action.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  The number of days an
application is in review with the division shall be calculated from the day
completeness is determined under K.C.C. 20.20.033 to the date a final decision
is issued on the permit application.  The number of days shall be calculated by
counting every calendar day and excludes the following periods:       1.  Any
period between the day that the division notifies the applicant in writing that
additional information is required to further process the application and the
day when responsive information is submitted by the applicant.  If the county
determines that corrections, studies, or other information submitted by the
applicant is insufficient, it shall notify the applicant of the deficiencies
and the procedures of this section shall apply as if a new request for
information had been made.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                a.  The division shall set a sixty-day
deadline for the submittal of corrections, studies, or other information, and
shall provide written notification to the applicant.  The division may extend
the deadline upon receipt of a written request from an applicant providing
satisfactory justification for an extension;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  The period during which an
environmental impact statement is being prepared following a determination of
significance under chapter 43.21C RCW, as established in K.C.C. 20.44.050;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  Any period between issuance
of an administrative decision and resolution of an administrative appeal, and
any additional period provided by the administrative appeal decision;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  Any period during which an
applicant fails to post the property, if required by this chapter, following
the date notice is required until an affidavit of posting is provided to the division
by the applicant;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              5.  Any time extension mutually
agreed upon by the applicant and the division; </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              6.  Any time during which there
is an outstanding fee balance;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              7.  The period during which the
state Department of Ecology is reviewing and approving shoreline variance or
shoreline conditional use permit, measured between the date of filing with the
state Department of Ecology and the date the county receives the state Department
of Ecology's decision;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              8.  The period during which
another agency is reviewing and issuing a decision on any required modification
to a previous approval, after the required construction permit has been deemed
complete; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              9.  The period during which
information has been requested that can only be obtained during a seasonal
window, including but not limited to, infiltration mounding analysis, traffic
studies when school is in session, wetland studies in the growing season, or
stream typing or fish status outside of times of low flow.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D.  The county shall provide any
written comments and requests for corrections within thirty days of each
submittal or resubmittal.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            E.  The division may cancel a
permit application in the following situations:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.    Failure by the applicant to
submit corrections, studies, or other information acceptable to the division
after two rounds of written requests under subsection C.. of this; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Failure by the applicant to
make full payment of review fees within sixty days of the division's invoice.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            F.  If an applicant informs the
division in writing that the applicant would like to temporarily suspend the
review of the project for more than sixty days, or if an applicant is not
responsive for more than sixty consecutive days after the division has notified
the applicant in writing, that additional information is required to further
process the application, an additional thirty days may be added to the time
periods for the division to issue a final decision.  Any written notice from
the division to the applicant that additional information is required to
further process the application shall include a notice that nonresponsiveness
for sixty consecutive days may result in thirty days being added to the time
period for permit review.  For the purposes of this subsection,
&quot;nonresponsiveness&quot; means that an applicant is not making
demonstrable progress on providing additional requested information to the
division, or that there is not ongoing communication from the applicant to the
division on the applicant's ability or willingness to provide the additional
information.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            G.  The time periods for permit
review established in this section shall not apply if an unforeseen event, as
determined by the permitting division manager, disrupts normal county
operations and prevents permit review from being feasible.  This could include,
but is not limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Declared emergencies under
K.C.C. chapter 12.52;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Court orders, litigation, or
settlements pursuant to specific applications or to the King County
Comprehensive Plan or the county's development regulations;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  Building or land use
moratoria;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  A recession resulting in
unexpected staffing changes; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              5.  Denial of service for
critical infrastructure, such as software failure, breach or termination of
contract, loss of internet access, or cyber security breach.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            H.  The time periods established in
subsection A. of this section shall not apply if a proposed development or
application:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Requires either modification
or waiver of a development regulation as part of a demonstration project in
K.C.C. chapter 21A.55.  Any time periods for permit review in that chapter
shall apply;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Involves uses or activities
related to mineral extraction or processing, coal mining, materials processing
facilities, or fossil fuel facilities: or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  Is to rectify a code
violation case;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              I.1.  If an application is
revised by the applicant</span> <span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>and
results in a substantial change in the application review, the division may, in
some cases, need to restart the time period for permit review in K.C.C.
20.20.100.   A substantial change in the application review may also include
new public notice and revised vesting.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  For the purposes of this
subsection I.:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                a.  a &quot;substantial
change&quot; that results in new public notice, revised vesting, and a restart
of the time periods for permit review, includes, but is not limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                  (1)  the creation of
additional lots;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                  (2).  the reduction or
elimination of open space; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                  (3)  a change in land use;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                b.  a &quot;substantial
change&quot; that results in a restart of the time periods for permit review,
includes, but is not limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                  (1)  a fifty-percent or more
increase to the total value of building construction work, including all finish
work, painting, roofing, electrical, plumbing, heating, ventilation, air
conditioning, elevators, fire systems, and any other permanent fixtures;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                  (2)  a ten-percent or more
increase to the total building square footage, impervious surface area,
parking, or building height;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                  (3)  when a change to the
application results in a change to the type of drainage review required under
the King County Surface Water Design Manual, if the new type of drainage review
adds additional requirements;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                  (4)  when a change to the
application results in a new requirement for a road variance under K.C.C. Title
14;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                  (5)  when a change in points
of ingress or egress to the property, where:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                    (a) the ingress or egress
point is moved more than 25 percent of the width of the property line width on
the same street; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                    (b) the ingress or egress
point is on a different street; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                  (6) when a change to the
application results in a new or increased impact to critical areas that will
require a revision to mitigation measures..</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            J.  The division shall require that
all plats, short plats, building permits, clearing and grading permits,
conditional use permits, special use permits, commercial site development
permits, shoreline substantial development permits, or binding site plans,
issued for development activities on or within five hundred feet of designated
agricultural lands, forest lands, or mineral resource lands, contain a notice
that the subject property is within or near designated agricultural lands,
forest lands, or mineral resource lands, on which a variety of commercial
activities may occur that are not compatible with residential development for
certain periods of limited duration.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            K.  Where federal, state, or county
law requires a shorter time period for permit review and decision, the division
should comply with the shorter time periods.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            L.1.  An applicant may, in writing
and with the division's agreement, voluntarily opt out of the time period for
permit review and decision specified in this section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  If an applicant is under an
application processing service agreement with the county, the time periods for
permit review in that agreement shall control.  (Ord. 19881 § 64, 2024:  Ord.
19872 § 20, 2024:  Ord. 19648 § 10, 2023:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black'>Ord. 19034 <span style='letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>§</span> 6, 2019:  Ord. 18230 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span>
117, 2016:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 16950 </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>11,
2010:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord.
16263 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 8, 2008:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 15170 <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 3, 2005:  Ord. 14788 <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span>7, 2003:  Ord. 13250 § 1, 1998:  Ord.
13097 § 3, 1998:  Ord. 12627 § 5, 1997:  Ord. 12273 § 2, 1996:  Ord. 12196 §
17, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.20.105  Permit
extension.</b>  Upon written request to the department made by the applicant
before the expiration of a permit for a conditional use, variance, alteration
exception or reasonable use exception, the department may extend the period of
the permit for one year if:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A. 
Regulations governing the approval of the land use decision have not changed;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Site
conditions have not significantly changed in a manner that would have affected
the original permit approval; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  The
applicant pays applicable permit extension fees.  (</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black'>Ord. 19691 <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 2, 2023:  Ord. 19393 <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 1, 2022:  </span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 16515 </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
4, 2009).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.20.120  Permit
processing guides - production - availability.</b>  The department shall produce
guides describing permit processing, including making an appeal or
participating in a hearing.  The department shall make them available to the
public and shall post them to its website.  (Ord. 19881 § 65, 2024:  Ord. 12196
§ 19, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.20.130  Reports
on permit review timelines.</b>  Reporting on permit review timelines shall be
submitted annually to the council, in accordance with K.C.C. 20.20.135.  (Ord.
19872 § 21, 2024).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>20.20.135  Permit
review performance reports - transmittal of ordinance that implements
recommendations.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  The
executive shall, in consultation and collaboration with councilmembers, prepare
a permit review performance report.  The executive should transmit the report
to the council by December 31, 2025, in the form of an electronic copy with the
clerk of the council, who shall retain an electronic copy and provide an
electronic copy to all councilmembers, the council chief of staff, and the lead
staff for the local services and land use committee or its successor.  The
report, shall include, but not be limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  A
description of the current requirements for project review timeline reporting
in state and county law;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  A plan
for, and the resources needed to, comply with the project review timeline
reporting requirements in chapter 36.70B RCW;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  A plan
for, and the resources needed to, provide project review timeline reporting for
all project permit types, similar to the reporting requirement for housing
projects in chapter 36.70B RCW;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  A plan
for, and the resources needed to, provide project review timeline reporting on
the county's website;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  An
evaluation of the impacts of the project review timelines established in
chapter 36.70B RCW on customer service for county residences, including:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  a
description of how the additional staffing, fee increase, code changes, and
process improvements will be demonstrable to a resident applying for a project
permit; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a. 
whether additional investments, code change, or process improvements will be
needed to comply with the requirements in RCW 36.70B or potential growth in
project permit applications in the 2026-2027 or future biennia;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              6. 
Recommendations for any code changes to address the evaluation required by this
proviso, which should include a requirement that the annual reports are
required no later than June of each year;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  The
executive shall transmit an ordinance that implements the recommendations
required by the report in this section, as well as the first annual report, to
the council by June 30, 2026, in the form of an electronic copy with the clerk
of the council, who shall retain an electronic copy and provide an electronic
copy to all councilmembers, the council chief of staff, and the lead staff for
the local services and land use committee or its successor.  The ordinance
shall, at a minimum, amend K.C.C. 20.20.130, to include the required reporting
components.  (Ord. 19872 § 26, 2024).</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'>&nbsp;</p>

